          A common flaw in naive text clean-up utilities is checking only for zero-length strings (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">line.length === 0</code>). In real-world documents, lines that appear completely blank visually often contain invisible non-printing characters:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
          <li><strong>Trailing Indentation Spaces:</strong> Lines containing only spaces (e.g. <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">"    "</code>) left behind by code editors or word processor indentations.</li>
          <li><strong>Tab Characters:</strong> Lines consisting of a single tab (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">\t</code>) copied from spreadsheet grids or code snippets.</li>
          <li><strong>Carriage Return Artifacts:</strong> Windows CRLF (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">\r\n</code>) vs Unix LF (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">\n</code>) line-ending mismatches where an isolated carriage return lingers.</li>
